### Display width utilities

Approximate terminal column widths for each tier. Needed by layout
algorithms to compute proper alignment:

- ASCII: len() — each char is 1 column
- Unicode: heuristic — most symbols 1 col, CJK 2 col
- Emoji: heuristic — base emoji 2 cols, modifiers 0 cols

### State-to-glyph bridge functions

Convenience functions that map execution state names to GlyphIds.
These are the bridge between Layer 2 (progress state machine) and
Layer 1 (glyph vocabulary):

```rust
pub fn node_state_glyph(state: &str) -> GlyphId { ... }
pub fn edge_state_glyph(state: &str) -> GlyphId { ... }
pub fn dag_phase_glyph(phase: &str) -> GlyphId { ... }
```

### Why in core/ir?

The glyph module belongs in core/ir (not in a renderer crate) because:
- It's a vocabulary, not a rendering engine — any crate can use it
- The language module already models format-specific visual concerns
  (comment syntax, naming conventions) in core/ir
- CI context, error messages, and future tools will all want consistent
  glyph references without depending on a renderer crate
- GlyphId + GlyphSet follow the same pattern as TypeId + TypeRegistry

