### Carriage-return rendering loop

```rust
pub struct TerminalRenderer {
    layout: DagLayout,       // Computed from DagSnapshot
    glyphs: &'static GlyphSet,
    tier: Tier,              // Which encoding to use
    width: u16,              // Terminal width
    line_count: usize,       // Lines in current display (for cursor-up)
}

impl TerminalRenderer {
    fn render_frame(&mut self) {
        // Move cursor back to top of display area
        if self.line_count > 0 {
            // \x1b[{N}A = cursor up N lines, \r = carriage return
            print!("\x1b[{}A\r", self.line_count);
        }

        // Build display lines
        let lines = self.layout.render_lines(&self.glyphs, self.tier);
        self.line_count = lines.len();

        // Print each line, clearing to end of line (\x1b[K) to
        // handle variable-width content cleanly
        for line in &lines {
            println!("{}\x1b[K", line);
        }
    }
}
```

The key insight: `\r` + cursor-up rewrites the same screen area on
every state change. Between node executions, the terminal shows the
updated DAG with the current node's glyph changing from ⏳→🔄→✅.
When execution completes, the final state stays on screen as static
text.

### Layout algorithm

Leveled layout based on topological distance from root:

    1. Compute topo order
    2. Assign each node a "level" = max distance from any root
    3. Within each level, order nodes by topo index
    4. Render left-to-right, wrapping at terminal width
    5. Draw edges using glyph system connectors:
       - Same-level: ConnectorHorizontal between nodes
       - Cross-level: ConnectorVertical + ConnectorCorner

For small DAGs (≤8 nodes), single-line horizontal layout:

    ✅ A ──── ✅ B ──── 🔄 C ──── ⏳ D

For larger DAGs, multi-row with vertical connections:

    ✅ A ──┬── ✅ B ──── ✅ D ──┬── ⏳ F
           │                    │
           └── ✅ C ────────────┘
                       │
                       └── 🔄 E

### Why carriage return (not a TUI framework)?

- Zero dependencies — just print/println with ANSI escape sequences
- Works perfectly for our use case: a fixed-size display that updates
  between synchronous node executions
- The display is not interactive — no scrolling, input, or mouse
- When piped (not a TTY), falls back to progressive line output
  (no cursor movement, just append)
- If interactivity is ever needed, the DagProgress model works with
  any renderer — swap in a TUI framework later without changing the
  data layer

